//
// Test: Instruction buffer size limit (~4 MiB assumed, proved wrong) in xclbin vs ELF flow.
//
// Previous agents thought the XRT firmware imposed a ~4 MiB limit on instruction buffer objects (bos)
// used by the xclbin flow. This test proves that assumption wrong.
//
// Four configurations are tested:
//   (1) xclbin flow, instr buffer padded to ~3.8 MiB  → PASS
//   (2) xclbin flow, instr buffer padded to ~4.2 MiB  → FAIL (expected -- but does not actually fail!)
//   (3) ELF flow, ELF padded to ~3.8 MiB              → PASS
//   (4) ELF flow, ELF padded to ~4.2 MiB              → PASS
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// Compile for xclbin flow (generates aie.xclbin + insts.bin):
// RUN: %python aiecc.py --no-aiesim --no-xchesscc --no-xbridge --aie-generate-xclbin --aie-generate-npu-insts --no-compile-host --xclbin-name=aie.xclbin --npu-insts-name=insts.bin %S/aie.mlir
//
// Compile for ELF flow (generates aie.elf):
// RUN: %PYTHON aiecc.py --generate-full-elf --no-xchesscc --no-xbridge %S/aie_elf.mlir
//
// Build host executables:
// RUN: g++ %S/test_xclbin.cpp -o test_xclbin.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags -lrt -lstdc++ %test_utils_flags
// RUN: g++ %S/test_elf.cpp -o test_elf.exe -std=c++17 -Wall %xrt_flags -lrt -lstdc++
//
// --- (1) xclbin flow, below 4 MiB limit (expect PASS) ---
// RUN: ./test_xclbin.exe -x aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i insts.bin --pad-to-bytes 4000000 -v 1
//
// --- (2) xclbin flow, above 4 MiB limit (expect FAIL) ---
// RUN: ! ./test_xclbin.exe -x aie.xclbin -k MLIR_AIE -i insts.bin --pad-to-bytes 4200000 -v 1
//
// --- (3) ELF flow, below 4 MiB (expect PASS — no BO limit in ELF flow) ---
// RUN: ./test_elf.exe --elf aie.elf --pad-to-bytes 4000000 -v 1
//
// --- (4) ELF flow, above 4 MiB (expect PASS — no BO limit in ELF flow) ---
// RUN: ./test_elf.exe --elf aie.elf --pad-to-bytes 4200000 -v 1
